This is a known breaking change. Android deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` (API 30) and .NET 10 MAUI removed the corresponding `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize`. Here's how to handle it:

### 1. Use MAUI's built-in `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`

MAUI has a built-in auto-scroll mechanism (on by default since .NET 8). Make sure you haven't disabled it:

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs — do NOT disable this:
// .ConfigureMauiHandlers(h => h.AddHandler<Entry, EntryHandler>(
//     nameof(Entry), _ => KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect()));
```

If your `Entry`/`Editor` is inside a `ScrollView`, the framework will automatically scroll the focused input into view.

### 2. Chat-screen pattern: use `Margin` + keyboard insets

For a "pinned bottom bar" layout, the auto-scroll alone isn't enough. Adjust your layout's bottom padding/margin when the keyboard appears:

**Android — `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:**
```csharp
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
        Window!.DecorView, new KeyboardInsetsListener(this));
}

class KeyboardInsetsListener(Activity activity)
    : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(
        Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Bottom padding = keyboard height minus nav bar
        int bottomPx = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom - navInsets.Bottom, 0);

        // Send to MAUI on the main thread
        MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(() =>
            MessagingCenter.Send<object, double>(
                activity, "KeyboardHeightChanged",
                bottomPx / DeviceDisplay.MainDisplayInfo.Density));

        return ViewCompat.OnApplyWindowInsets(v, insets);
    }
}
```

**In your MAUI page:**
```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" x:Name="MessageList" ... />
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" x:Name="InputBar"
                            Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand"/>
        <Button Text="Send"/>
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

```csharp
protected override void OnAppearing()
{
    base.OnAppearing();
    MessagingCenter.Subscribe<object, double>(
        this, "KeyboardHeightChanged", (_, height) =>
            InputBar.Margin = new Thickness(0, 0, 0, height));
}
```

### 3. AndroidManifest.xml

Ensure your activity uses `adjustResize` (still helps with `WindowInsets` delivery):

```xml
<activity android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" ... />
```

### iOS

iOS handles this automatically with MAUI's `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`. No extra work needed — the platform uses `UIScrollView` content inset adjustment under the hood.

### TL;DR

| .NET 9 | .NET 10 |
|---|---|
| `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` | Listen to `WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()` and adjust layout margin |

The `WindowInsets` approach is future-proof and works with Android 15's mandatory edge-to-edge mode.
